1
00:00:01,550 --> 00:00:04,629
Every morning, your AI wakes up
brilliant, and blank.

2
00:00:04,663 --> 00:00:08,088
It has read more than any human
who ever lived.

3
00:00:08,122 --> 00:00:10,163
But it has never met you.

4
00:00:10,198 --> 00:00:15,006
It doesn't know your style guide, your codebase
rules, or the way your team

5
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:15,698
writes reports.

6
00:00:15,732 --> 00:00:18,465
So Anthropic built a way to teach it.

7
00:00:18,499 --> 00:00:19,157
Not retraining.

8
00:00:19,191 --> 00:00:20,194
Not fine tuning.

9
00:00:20,229 --> 00:00:20,886
A folder.

10
00:00:20,921 --> 00:00:26,075
And by the end of this video, you'll know
how to teach an AI things

11
00:00:26,109 --> 00:00:27,804
it was never trained on.

12
00:00:38,839 --> 00:00:41,438
Here's the problem skills exist to solve.

13
00:00:41,476 --> 00:00:43,321
Every conversation starts from zero.

14
00:00:43,359 --> 00:00:45,581
So you paste the same instructions.

15
00:00:45,619 --> 00:00:45,958
Again.

16
00:00:45,995 --> 00:00:46,711
And again.

17
00:00:46,749 --> 00:00:48,218
Follow our brand colors.

18
00:00:48,255 --> 00:00:49,724
Use our report format.

19
00:00:49,762 --> 00:00:51,607
Never touch the legacy folder.

20
00:00:51,645 --> 00:00:56,881
You are re-teaching a genius the same lesson every
single day, like it's stuck

21
00:00:56,918 --> 00:00:58,387
in a time loop.

22
00:00:58,425 --> 00:01:02,530
There had to be a better way to make
knowledge stick.

23
00:01:07,768 --> 00:01:12,460
A skill is that better way, and it's almost
embarrassingly simple.

24
00:01:12,503 --> 00:01:13,751
It's a folder.

25
00:01:13,794 --> 00:01:17,194
Inside, one file called SKILL dot M D.

26
00:01:17,237 --> 00:01:22,360
At the top, a name, and one sentence
describing when to use

27
00:01:22,403 --> 00:01:22,790
it.

28
00:01:22,833 --> 00:01:27,955
Below that, the actual expertise, written
exactly like you'd train a new

29
00:01:27,998 --> 00:01:28,386
employee.

30
00:01:28,429 --> 00:01:29,246
Step one.

31
00:01:29,290 --> 00:01:30,107
Step two.

32
00:01:30,150 --> 00:01:31,829
Watch out for this.

33
00:01:31,872 --> 00:01:33,120
And that's it.

34
00:01:33,163 --> 00:01:34,412
No code required.

35
00:01:34,455 --> 00:01:38,716
If you can write instructions, you
can teach an AI.

36
00:01:43,959 --> 00:01:47,218
But here's the part that makes skills
genuinely clever.

37
00:01:47,254 --> 00:01:49,781
The agent doesn't read all your skills.

38
00:01:49,818 --> 00:01:51,978
It reads a shelf of labels.

39
00:01:52,015 --> 00:01:55,273
Just each skill's name, and that one
line description.

40
00:01:55,310 --> 00:01:56,738
A few words each.

41
00:01:56,775 --> 00:02:01,864
Then, when your request matches one of those
labels, it pulls the whole book

42
00:02:01,901 --> 00:02:04,794
down and reads it, right at that moment.

43
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:06,258
It's called progressive disclosure.

44
00:02:06,295 --> 00:02:11,385
You can install a hundred skills, and they cost
almost nothing, until the exact

45
00:02:11,421 --> 00:02:12,849
second one is needed.

46
00:02:16,086 --> 00:02:19,250
And a skill can carry more than words.

47
00:02:19,291 --> 00:02:22,054
Drop reference files next to the
instructions.

48
00:02:22,094 --> 00:02:23,657
Your full brand book.

49
00:02:23,697 --> 00:02:24,458
A template.

50
00:02:24,498 --> 00:02:25,259
Example outputs.

51
00:02:25,299 --> 00:02:26,461
Even runnable scripts.

52
00:02:26,501 --> 00:02:29,265
Which means a skill isn't just advice.

53
00:02:29,305 --> 00:02:34,472
It can hand the agent an actual tool, and the
instructions for using

54
00:02:34,512 --> 00:02:36,074
it, in one package.

55
00:02:36,114 --> 00:02:38,077
Knowledge and machinery, zipped together.

56
00:02:43,317 --> 00:02:45,422
Let's build one, right now.

57
00:02:45,465 --> 00:02:49,719
Say your team keeps shipping documents
that break brand rules.

58
00:02:49,762 --> 00:02:52,297
Create a folder called brand guardian.

59
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:57,453
In SKILL dot M D, the description reads, use
this whenever creating

60
00:02:57,496 --> 00:03:00,031
or reviewing any customer facing document.

61
00:03:00,074 --> 00:03:01,750
Below it, the rules.

62
00:03:01,793 --> 00:03:02,180
Colors.

63
00:03:02,223 --> 00:03:02,610
Fonts.

64
00:03:02,652 --> 00:03:03,039
Tone.

65
00:03:03,082 --> 00:03:05,188
The words we never use.

66
00:03:05,231 --> 00:03:06,477
Save the file.

67
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:08,195
That's the whole install.

68
00:03:08,238 --> 00:03:09,055
Now watch.

69
00:03:09,098 --> 00:03:14,211
Ask the agent to draft a landing page,
and it checks the

70
00:03:14,254 --> 00:03:19,367
shelf, sees brand guardian, matches, loads it,
and every rule you wrote

71
00:03:19,410 --> 00:03:21,945
is suddenly just how it works.

72
00:03:25,188 --> 00:03:28,020
And that same folder format works
everywhere Claude runs.

73
00:03:28,052 --> 00:03:32,793
In Claude Code, drop it in your project, and your
whole team inherits it with

74
00:03:32,825 --> 00:03:33,747
a git pull.

75
00:03:33,779 --> 00:03:38,202
In the Claude apps, upload the same folder and
it works in your chats.

76
00:03:38,234 --> 00:03:42,338
And through the A P I, your production
agents can load it too.

77
00:03:42,370 --> 00:03:45,202
One format, written once, installed
wherever you need it.

78
00:03:50,434 --> 00:03:55,636
Step back, and you can see the machine we've
been assembling across

79
00:03:55,680 --> 00:03:56,511
this series.

80
00:03:56,554 --> 00:03:59,134
Subagents gave your AI more brains.

81
00:03:59,177 --> 00:04:03,505
M C P gave it hands on the real world.

82
00:04:03,549 --> 00:04:05,691
And skills give it crafts.

83
00:04:05,735 --> 00:04:07,440
Trained, specific, expert crafts.

84
00:04:07,484 --> 00:04:12,249
One agent, spawning helpers, reaching real
systems, carrying your team's entire

85
00:04:12,292 --> 00:04:12,686
playbook.

86
00:04:12,730 --> 00:04:14,872
That's not a chatbot anymore.

87
00:04:14,915 --> 00:04:16,183
That's a colleague.

88
00:04:21,427 --> 00:04:23,985
One warning before you go skill shopping.

89
00:04:24,022 --> 00:04:28,805
A skill is instructions your agent will
trust, and code it may run.

90
00:04:28,842 --> 00:04:32,884
Install one from a stranger, and you've
handed them the keys.

91
00:04:32,921 --> 00:04:36,591
A poisoned skill can quietly rewrite how
your agent behaves.

92
00:04:36,628 --> 00:04:39,187
So treat skills like you treat software.

93
00:04:39,224 --> 00:04:41,411
Read them before you install them.

94
00:04:41,448 --> 00:04:42,524
They're just text.

95
00:04:42,561 --> 00:04:43,636
Open the folder.

96
00:04:43,673 --> 00:04:45,119
It takes one minute.

97
00:04:48,156 --> 00:04:48,917
A folder.

98
00:04:48,957 --> 00:04:50,920
A label on a shelf.

99
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:54,525
Expertise that loads at the exact
moment it's needed.

100
00:04:54,565 --> 00:04:58,531
That's skills, and your AI is no
longer just smart.

101
00:04:58,571 --> 00:04:59,332
It's trained.

102
00:04:59,372 --> 00:05:03,738
Now, everything you've learned in this series
has been leading somewhere.

103
00:05:03,778 --> 00:05:08,945
Next time, we take all of it, the loop, the
tools, the permissions,

104
00:05:08,985 --> 00:05:12,550
and we build our own Claude Code,
from scratch.

105
00:05:12,590 --> 00:05:14,553
The whole machine, one video.

106
00:05:14,593 --> 00:05:17,357
Subscribe to codewired, and get wired in.
